From: Custos OS build
Subject: [PATCH] hotplug: do not kill the daemon when there is no USB stack

pcscd runs its USB hotplug thread whenever it was built with libusb, and
libusb is pulled in by the ccid package whether or not a USB reader is ever
used. On a device whose kernel has no USB support at all there is no usbfs
for libusb to find, libusb_init() fails, and pcscd kills its own process:

    Log2(PCSC_LOG_CRITICAL, "libusb_init failed: %s", ...);
    /* emergency exit */
    kill(getpid(), SIGTERM);

That is a sensible default on a desktop, where every reader is USB and a dead
libusb means the daemon can do nothing useful. It is wrong here. The SEC1210
is a *serial* CCID reader, declared in reader.conf.d and already initialised
by the time this thread runs; upstream's suicide throws away a working reader
and every client then fails with SCARD_E_NO_SERVICE (0x8010001D) because no
daemon is left to talk to.

The kill() is also load-bearing in a way that is easy to miss, so it cannot
simply be deleted. HPSearchHotPluggables() creates the pipe, starts this
thread, and then blocks:

    /* Wait for initial readers to setup */
    if (read(pipefd[0], &c, 1) == -1)

Returning early without writing to that pipe would leave the daemon hung at
startup rather than dead - the same SCARD_E_NO_SERVICE, harder to diagnose.
So write the handshake byte first, exactly as the success path does, and only
then stop the thread. The caller carries on with the readers it already has,
USB hotplug is simply absent, and serial readers keep working.

--- a/src/hotplug_libusb.c
+++ b/src/hotplug_libusb.c
@@ -483,8 +483,22 @@
 	if (r < 0)
 	{
 		Log2(PCSC_LOG_CRITICAL, "libusb_init failed: %s", libusb_error_name(r));
-		/* emergency exit */
-		kill(getpid(), SIGTERM);
+
+		/*
+		 * No USB stack on this system. Serial readers from reader.conf.d
+		 * have already been initialised and remain usable, so keep the
+		 * daemon alive and simply do without hotplug.
+		 */
+		Log1(PCSC_LOG_INFO,
+			"no USB support: continuing with serial readers only");
+
+		/*
+		 * HPSearchHotPluggables() is blocked reading this pipe. It must be
+		 * released or the daemon hangs at startup instead of finishing it.
+		 */
+		if (write(pipefd[1], &c, 1) == -1)
+			Log2(PCSC_LOG_ERROR, "write: %s", strerror(errno));
+
 		return NULL;
 	}
 
